# Line endings are pinned to LF for everything the toolchain reads.
#
# This is not style. On Windows, git converts to CRLF on checkout by default, so
# a run's isolated worktree gets CRLF sources while the developer's tree has LF —
# and `gofmt -l` reports every converted file as needing a rewrite. The gate then
# refuses every run with a red format check over a tree nobody edited, and the
# fleet stalls with a message that describes the wrong problem entirely.
*.go      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# The site tree is served as files, so what git stores is what a browser gets.
# Left to git's own guess on Windows, an HTML or CSS file checked out with CRLF
# and committed back differs from the same file on a Linux runner, and the diff
# somebody then reviews is every line of a page nobody edited.
*.html    text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.tsv     text eol=lf

# Images marked binary so git never applies eol conversion to them: a JPEG with
# 0x0a bytes rewritten to 0x0d0a is a corrupt file that still has its extension,
# and it fails at the browser rather than at the commit. The *.webp rule records
# how such a file would be stored if one ever reached the tree; it is not
# permission to commit one — S1-006 fixes the admitted formats at jpeg, png and
# gif, and S1-003 refuses any other extension under img/.
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.png     binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
